---
<section class="es-section" id="timeline">
  <p class="es-eyebrow">Follow the Changes</p><h2>Gibson ES-330 Timeline</h2>
  <p>Start with features built into the guitar, then use removable parts to support the date. An inlay change or neck-joint position usually tells you more than a knob that can be swapped in minutes.</p>
  <article class="es-change" id="introduction"><p class="es-years">1959</p><h3>Dots, Black P-90s and a Trapeze</h3>
    <p>The ES-330 arrives as a fully hollow thinline with a laminated body, mahogany neck and bound rosewood fingerboard. Early examples have dot inlays, black plastic dogear P-90 covers, nickel hardware, gold bonnet knobs and the 16th-fret body joint. The two-pickup TD has four controls and a selector; the single-pickup T has two controls.</p>
    <div class="es-photos"><ESPhoto src={dot} id="early-controls" title="Early ES-330 Layout" alt="Dot-neck ES-330 with four gold bonnet knobs, black P-90 covers and a trapeze" caption="The gold bonnet knobs have rounded tops without metal reflector inserts. The trapeze belongs on an early ES-330; it is not evidence of a mid-1960s build." /><ESPhoto src={dots} id="dot-inlay" title="Small Dot Inlays" alt="Round dot inlay and bound rosewood fingerboard on an early ES-330" caption="Round dots identify the early fingerboard style. Check the FON and construction to separate an original from a later dot-neck reissue." /></div>
    <p class="es-meaning"><strong>What It Means for an ES-330:</strong> This combination points toward the early production style. A full-feeling neck alone cannot establish 1959; the FON is a more useful check on an early example.</p>
  </article>
  <article class="es-change" id="early-sixties"><p class="es-years">1960 to 1961</p><h3>Reflector Knobs and Changing Neck Profiles</h3>
    <p>Dot inlays and black pickup covers continue. Reflector knobs begin appearing around 1960, and many necks become shallower through the early 1960s. Neck depth varies between instruments, so there is no measurement that reliably separates every 1959, 1960 and 1961 guitar.</p>
    <div class="es-photos"><ESPhoto src={reflectors} id="reflector-knobs" title="Gold Reflector Knobs" alt="Gold ES-330 knobs with metal inserts marked Volume and Tone" caption="The flat metal insert distinguishes a reflector from a bonnet. This photo shows the style, not a claim that the guitar was made in the first year it appeared." /><ESPhoto src={earlyBridge} id="no-wire-bridge" title="Early No-Wire ABR-1" alt="Close-up of an ES-330 ABR-1 bridge with metal saddles and no retaining wire" caption="Early bridges have no saddle-retaining wire. A missing wire on a later bridge is not the same thing, so inspect the bridge construction as well." /></div>
    <p class="es-meaning"><strong>What It Means for an ES-330:</strong> Reflectors plus dots still belong within the early period. Use R for a 1960 FON and Q for 1961; impressed headstock serials enter the picture in 1961.</p>
  </article>
  <article class="es-change" id="blocks-and-covers"><p class="es-years">1962 to 1964</p><h3>Small Blocks and Metal Pickup Covers</h3>
    <p>Small block inlays replace dots during 1962. Black plastic P-90 covers give way to metal covers around late 1962 into 1963. These are separate changes: a 1962 block-neck ES-330 can still have black covers. Do not reject that combination just because later block-neck guitars usually have metal covers.</p>
    <div class="es-photos"><ESPhoto src={blocks} id="block-inlays" title="Small Block Inlays" alt="Small rectangular inlays on a Gibson ES-330 rosewood fingerboard" caption="Blocks establish the later fingerboard style, beginning during 1962. They remain in use across several years and also appear on modern guitars." /><ESPhoto src={metalP90} id="metal-p90" title="Metal Dogear Cover" alt="Metal dogear P-90 cover with six pole screws on a cherry ES-330" caption="The plated cover changes the pickup’s appearance, not its single-coil design. This is still a P-90, not a PAF or Patent Number humbucker." /></div>
    <p>Nickel hardware belongs to the early part of this period. Reflector knobs remain a familiar feature, with color depending on the finish and specification. Nylon saddles appear on 1960s bridges, but saddle material and retaining-wire construction should not be used as a one-year cutoff.</p>
    <p class="es-meaning"><strong>What It Means for an ES-330:</strong> Blocks with black covers can be transitional. Blocks with metal covers cover a much wider period, so the serial, nut width and neck joint still need checking.</p>
  </article>
  <article class="es-change" id="mid-sixties"><p class="es-years">1965 to 1967</p><h3>Narrower Nuts and Chrome Hardware</h3>
    <p>Around 1965, narrower nuts and chrome-plated hardware become common. The change overlaps: some guitars retain a wider nut or nickel parts. Earlier nut widths are often near 1 11/16 inches, while many later examples are near 1 9/16 inches. Intermediate widths also exist.</p>
    <div class="es-photos"><ESPhoto src={cherry} id="cherry-block-neck" title="Mid-1960s ES-330 Features" alt="Cherry ES-330 with small blocks, metal pickup covers, black reflector knobs and nylon saddles" caption="This cherry example brings the block inlays, metal P-90 covers, reflector knobs and trapeze together. Plating and nut width need closer inspection than a full-guitar photo can provide." /><ESPhoto src={nylonBridge} id="nylon-saddles" title="Nylon Bridge Saddles" alt="Pale nylon saddles fitted to an ES-330 Tune-o-Matic bridge" caption="Pale nylon saddles occur on 1960s ES-330s. They are replaceable wear parts, so their presence does not supply an exact guitar year." /></div>
    <p>Double-line Kluson stamps belong to the mid-1960s onward, with overlap around 1964 and 1965. Witch-hat knobs appear later in the decade, around 1967. The trapeze continues; unlike the ES-335, the ES-330 did not begin using it in 1965.</p>
    <p class="es-meaning"><strong>What It Means for an ES-330:</strong> A narrow nut, chrome parts and later controls help resolve an overlapping serial range. Measure nut width separately from neck depth: a narrow neck can still feel thick front to back.</p>
  </article>
  <article class="es-change" id="long-neck"><p class="es-years">Around 1968 to 1972</p><h3>The Later 19th-Fret Neck Joint</h3>
    <p>The later “long-neck” arrangement appears around 1968, moving the body joint from the 16th fret to the 19th. It exposes more of the fingerboard beyond the body and changes where the bridge and pickups sit relative to the body. It does not turn the ES-330 into a center-block guitar.</p>
    <div class="es-checks"><article><h3>Earlier 16th-Fret Joint</h3><p>The neck sits farther into the body. Dot-neck originals and many later block-neck examples use this layout, so blocks do not automatically mean a long neck.</p></article><article><h3>Later 19th-Fret Joint</h3><p>More frets are clear of the body. This supports a late-original-production date, but both vintage-style layouts have been revisited on later guitars.</p></article></div>
    <p><strong>Long neck does not mean long scale.</strong> Both layouts use Gibson’s nominal 24 3/4-inch scale. Count to the neck-to-body junction from the front; do not count where the curved cutaway reaches farthest up the fingerboard.</p>
    <p>Late examples can add laminated neck construction, a volute and, around 1970, a Made in USA stamp. Treat these as a group of supporting clues, not changes that occurred on every guitar together. The original ES-330TD run ends in 1972; a later serial needs to be checked as later production, a reissue or an unusual individual case.</p>
    <p class="es-meaning"><strong>What It Means for an ES-330:</strong> Check the actual joint on a guitar described as 1968. A seller’s “long neck” label is not enough to choose one year from an ambiguous serial.</p>
  </article>
</section>
<section class="es-section" id="neck-and-body"><p class="es-eyebrow">Read the Woodwork</p><h2>Neck Joint, Body and Headstock</h2>
  <div class="es-photos"><ESPhoto src={neck} id="short-neck-blocks" title="A Block-Neck With the Earlier Joint" alt="Block-inlay ES-330 neck meeting the body in the earlier short-neck arrangement" caption="This block-neck example still has the earlier body join. Inlay shape and neck-joint position need to be read independently." /><ESPhoto src={heel} id="neck-heel" title="Inspect the Heel From Behind" alt="Back of the neck heel and cutaways on an early Gibson ES-330" caption="Look for finish breaks, glue lines or altered heel contours that may indicate repair work. The rear view helps assess condition; use the front to count the joint fret." /><ESPhoto src={headstock} id="headstock" title="Plain ES-330 Headstock" alt="Unbound black ES-330 headstock with the Gibson logo and no crown inlay" caption="The familiar vintage ES-330 headstock is unbound and has no crown inlay. A plain headstock is normal for the model." /><ESPhoto src={tuners} id="oval-klusons" title="Single-Line Klusons With Oval Buttons" alt="Single-line Kluson Deluxe tuner casings with small white oval buttons on an early ES-330" caption="Read the casing stamp separately from the button shape. These small oval buttons are not the large tulip buttons used on some other Gibson models." /></div>
  <p>The hollow body has internal bracing, but not the ES-335’s full-length solid center block. Body binding is the cream-colored strip around the outer rim; the standard vintage ES-330’s f-hole edges are not separately bound. Rounded versus pointed cutaway horns can support an era, but body-shape variation is too broad to assign an exact year.</p>
  <p>A repaired neck or changed fingerboard can interrupt the expected pattern. Compare the <a href="/gibson-headstock-logo-chronology/">headstock logo chronology</a>, <a href="/gibson-tuner-dating-guide/">tuner dating guide</a> and <a href="/gibson-physical-features-hardware-guide/">physical features and hardware guide</a> before deciding what is original.</p>
</section>
<section class="es-section" id="pickups-and-hardware"><p class="es-eyebrow">Check the Parts in Context</p><h2>P-90s, Bridges, Tailpieces and Pickguards</h2>
  <div class="es-photos"><ESPhoto src={blackP90} id="black-p90s" title="Black Dogear P-90 Covers" alt="Black plastic ES-330 P-90 covers with mounting ears at both ends" caption="The mounting screws pass through the projecting ears at the ends. A soapbar P-90 instead has its two mounting screws through the middle of the cover, between the pole screws." /><ESPhoto src={trapeze} id="trapeze" title="Diamond Trapeze Tailpiece" alt="ES-330 trapeze tailpiece with a diamond on the crossbar, attached at the body end" caption="The strings anchor to a tailpiece attached at the body’s end. This design spans the ES-330’s original production run and cannot distinguish 1959 from 1965 by itself." /></div>
  <p>The ES-330’s metal-covered P-90 still has one coil. There is no universal ES-330 P-90 date stamp that replaces the guitar’s serial or FON. A replaced cover, rewind or replacement pickup can change what you see and hear without changing the guitar’s age.</p>
  <p>PAF and Patent Number humbucker dates do not apply to original ES-330 P-90s. If a guitar has humbuckers, first establish whether it is a later factory version or a modified vintage guitar. The <a href="https://www.joesvintageguitarsaz.com/post/gibson-paf-patent-number-pickup-guide/">PAF and Patent Number pickup guide</a> explains those different pickups; the <a href="/gibson-les-paul-dating-guide/">Les Paul guide</a> shows other P-90-equipped Gibson models.</p>
  <div class="es-checks"><article><h3>Bigsby or Other Vibrato</h3><p>A Bigsby can be a factory option or a later addition. Check mounting holes, finish shadows, paperwork and the exact vibrato type. Do not use an SG vibrato timeline as an ES-330 date chart.</p></article><article><h3>Added Stopbar</h3><p>A stopbar should not be assumed original on a standard vintage ES-330. Its hollow construction makes this different from converting a center-block ES-335. Have a qualified repairer assess the mounting and any internal reinforcement.</p></article></div>
  <div class="es-photos es-photos--single"><ESPhoto src={guard} id="pickguard-layers" title="Layered Pickguard Edge" alt="Alternating black and white layers on an ES-330 pickguard bevel" caption="Check the outline, bevel, screw positions and pickup cutouts together. A replacement can reproduce the visible layers, so layer count alone is not a date." /></div>
  <p>Use the <a href="/gibson-knob-dating-guide/">knob guide</a> for bonnet, reflector and witch-hat comparisons. Knobs, guards, saddles and tuner buttons are supporting evidence only when they belong to the guitar.</p>
</section>
